          "title": "Proceedings of the 2024 Conference on Empirical Methods in Natural Language Processing, pages 17716–17736",
          "review_excerpt": "Proceedings of the 2024 Conference on Empirical Methods in Natural Language Processing, pages 17716–17736\nNovember 12-16, 2024 ©2024 Association for Computational Linguistics\nSearching for Best Practices in Retrieval-Augmented Generation\nXi",
          "note": "verbatim excerpt only; no generated summary"
        },
        {
          "page": 2,
          "title": "In this study, we aim to identify the best practices",
          "review_excerpt": "In this study, we aim to identify the best practices\nfor RAG through extensive experimentation. Given\nthe infeasibility of testing all possible combinations\nof these methods, we adopt a three-step approach to\nidentify optimal RAG practices.",
          "note": "verbatim excerpt only; no generated summary"
        },
        {
          "page": 3,
          "title": "Large Language Model",
          "review_excerpt": "Large Language Model\nQuery Classification\nReranking\nDLM-based\nmonoT5\nmonoBERT\nRankLLaMA\nTILDE\n•\n•\n•\n•\n•\nRetrieval\nOriginal Query\nBM25\nContriever\nLLM-Embedder\nQuery Rewriting\nQuery Decomposition\nHyDE\nHybrid Search\nHyDE+Hybrid Search\n•\n•\n•\n•\n",
          "note": "verbatim excerpt only; no generated summary"
        },
        {
          "page": 4,
          "title": "To address this issue, we propose classifying",
          "review_excerpt": "To address this issue, we propose classifying\ntasks by type to determine if a query needs retrieval.\nWe categorize 15 tasks based on whether they pro-\nvide sufficient information, with specific tasks and\nexamples illustrated in Figure 2. Fo",
          "note": "verbatim excerpt only; no generated summary"
        },
        {
          "page": 5,
          "title": "methods using the LLM-Embedder recommended",
          "review_excerpt": "methods using the LLM-Embedder recommended\nin Section 3.2 as the query and document encoder:\n• Query Rewriting : Query rewriting refines\nqueries to better match relevant documents.\nInspired by the Rewrite-Retrieve-Read frame-\nwork (Ma et al",
          "note": "verbatim excerpt only; no generated summary"
        },
        {
          "page": 6,
          "title": "and “sides”. “Forward” repacks documents by de-",
          "review_excerpt": "and “sides”. “Forward” repacks documents by de-\nscending the relevancy scores from the reranking\nphase, whereas “reverse” arranges them in ascend-\ning order. Inspired by (Liu et al., 2024a), which\nconcluded that optimal performance is achie",
          "note": "verbatim excerpt only; no generated summary"
        },
        {
          "page": 7,
          "title": "4 Searching for Best RAG Practices",
          "review_excerpt": "4 Searching for Best RAG Practices\nIn the following section, we investigate the opti-\nmal practices for implementing RAG. To begin\nwith, we used the default practice identified in Sec-\ntion 3 for each module. Following the workflow\ndepicted",
          "note": "verbatim excerpt only; no generated summary"
        },
        {
          "page": 8,
          "title": "Method Commonsense Fact Check ODQA Multihop Med RAG Avg.",
          "review_excerpt": "Method Commonsense Fact Check ODQA Multihop Med RAG Avg.\nAcc Acc EM F1 Score EM F1 Score Acc Score Score F1 Latency\nwithout retrieval\n+ baseline 0.537 0.560 0.373 0.413 0.428 0.167 0.173 0.182 0.360 - 0.351 0.292 1.27\nclassification module ",
          "note": "verbatim excerpt only; no generated summary"
        },
        {
          "page": 9,
          "title": "module, implement the Hybrid method for retrieval,",
          "review_excerpt": "module, implement the Hybrid method for retrieval,\nuse TILDEv2 for reranking, opt for Reverse for\nrepacking, and employ Recomp for summarization.\nGiven that the retrieval module accounts for the\nmajority of processing time in the system, tr",
          "note": "verbatim excerpt only; no generated summary"
        },
        {
          "page": 10,
          "title": "Limitations",
          "review_excerpt": "Limitations\nWe have evaluated the impact of various methods\nfor fine-tuning LLM generators. Previous stud-\nies have demonstrated the feasibility of training\nboth the retriever and generator jointly. We would\nlike to explore this possibility",
          "note": "verbatim excerpt only; no generated summary"
        },
